\f2\b \cf2 The Most Pathetic Fight in Television History
\f1\b0 \
What followed was a physical confrontation that would have disappointed the choreographer of the most amateur community theater production. Alexander's idea of hand-to-hand combat appeared to have been learned from watching action movies while drunk, resulting in a fighting style that combined dramatic poses with complete ignorance of basic physics.\
Chanta's approach to violence was even less sophisticated, consisting primarily of flailing his arms while trying to maintain a safe distance from Alexander's equally wild swinging. The result was a conflict that resembled two inflatable tube men fighting during a windstorm.\
"Stand still so I can hit you properly!" Alexander demanded, launching what might generously be called a punch but which more accurately resembled an enthusiastic wave.\
"Stop trying to hit me!" Chanta replied, attempting to duck but instead performing what looked like an interpretive dance about confusion.\
They grappled briefly, which involved a great deal of grabbing each other's shirts and spinning in circles while making sounds that suggested both men were discovering muscles they had forgotten they possessed. Alexander attempted what he clearly believed was a devastating leg sweep but succeeded only in tripping himself, while Chanta's effort to push Alexander away resulted in both men falling backward into a decorative fountain.\
The fountain, designed to create a soothing water feature, was not intended to serve as a wrestling arena for two men with no practical experience in either wrestling or arena combat. Water splashed everywhere, creating a slippery surface that transformed their continued attempts at violence into something resembling competitive falling.\
"This is undignified!" Alexander complained, trying to maintain his television-host composure while soaking wet and covered in decorative pond weed.\
"You started it!" Chanta replied, attempting to stand up and immediately slipping back into the fountain with a splash that sent water cascading across the marble floor.\

\f2\b The Anticlimactic Resolution
\f1\b0 \
Their struggle continued for several more minutes, during which they managed to thoroughly soak the foyer, destroy several pieces of decorative statuary, and prove conclusively that neither man should ever consider a career in professional fighting. They rolled around in the fountain, fell out of the fountain, attempted to use fountain fixtures as weapons, and generally demonstrated why most conflicts should be resolved through conversation rather than violence.\
The engagement finally ended when both men, exhausted by their efforts and thoroughly soaked, found themselves sitting in opposite ends of the fountain, breathing heavily and glaring at each other with the intensity of people who had just discovered that physical confrontation was significantly more tiring than it appeared in movies.\
"You fight like a diseased penguin," Alexander panted.\
"You fight like a television host trying to fight," Chanta replied.\
They sat in the fountain for several more moments, both apparently coming to the realization that their conflict had accomplished nothing except providing an excellent demonstration of why civilised people used words instead of violence to resolve disputes.\
"This is ridiculous," Alexander finally said. "We're both intelligent adults. Surely we can reach some kind of agreement about Sarah's welfare without resorting to... whatever this was."\
Chanta considered this, water dripping from his hair. "You promise to treat her like a person instead of a television prop?"\
"I promise to... take her personal preferences into consideration when making decisions about her safety and our shared future."\
It wasn't quite the commitment Chanta had hoped for, but given that they were both sitting in a fountain after the most incompetent fight in recorded history, it seemed like progress.\
"Fine," Chanta said. "But if you hurt her, I'm hiring people who actually know how to fight."\
"Agreed," Alexander replied, struggling to his feet with whatever dignity he could muster while soaking wet and covered in fountain algae. "Now, can we please find my wife before something actually dangerous happens to her?"
